## 1. Introduction
During the past decade, porous coordination polymers (PCPs), best known as metalorganic frameworks (MOFs), have drawn growing attentions for applications in catalysis,1-6 photocatalysis,7-11 biomedicine,12-15 gas adsorption and separation,16-18 heavy metal adsorption,19-22 and degradation of nerve agents.11, 23, 24 MOFs are porous three-dimensional (3D) crystalline materials merging inorganic (metal ions/clusters) and organic linkers together.25-27 The solids exhibit ultrahigh surface area, low density, good conductivity, tunable structures and properties as well as low-toxicity, which are not available in the other materials.27, 28 The utilization of MOFs in heterogeneous photocatalysis29-33 is currently an emerging field in organic transformations,10, 30, 34-38 water oxidation,39 and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction,40-42 aiming the use of natural light as renewable energy to minimize the environmental problems. Although, there have been some limited reports relating the application of the MOFs as photocatalysts in organic reactions,30, 31, 43-47 with growing demand for organic chemicals and pharmaceuticals, it is urgent to develop green and effective strategies for selective synthesis of the compounds.
Fe(porphyrin) species in cytochrome P450 enzymes, which are belonging to a family of heme-containing proteins, play important role in the biological oxidation reactions.48-50 Synthetic metalloporphyrins as catalysts in the oxidation reactions can be rapidly deactivated due to molecular aggregation, formation of µ-oxo dimers, and self-oxidative degradation.51-54 Biological systems address these problems by protecting the heme-moiety within hierarchical structures of proteins/protein pockets. Recently, Yaghi and Zhou reported that the essential features of the protein pockets in cytochrome P450 can mimic by incorporating metalloporphyrin as building block within porous structure of PCN-222/MOF-545 $\mathbf{\mathrm{{PCN}=}}$ porous coordination network).55, 56 $\mathrm{PCN-222(Fe)/MOF{-}545{\mathrm{-}}F e}$ is a ultra-stable mesoporous porphyrinic zirconium MOF $[(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{FeTCPPCl})^{-4})_{2},$ , $\mathrm{FeTCPPCl}~=~5{,}10{,}15{,}20$ -tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)porphyrin-iron(III) chloride as a heme-like ligand]. The MOF contains hexagonal and triangular channels comprised of Fe-porphyrin-tetracarboxylate linkers connected to octameric $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O}{\bf H})_{4}({\bf O}{\bf H})_{4}({\bf H}_{2}{\bf O})_{4}$ clusters, prepared by solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ , FeTCPPCl, and benzoic acid as a modulator followed by treatment with HCl in DMF. Hydrochloric acid solution (the activation procedure) results in leaving the eight terminal - $\mathrm{{OH/H_{2}O}}$ groups which can act as ligands for post-synthetic modification,57 similar to that of the NU-1000.58, 59 Post-synthetic modification (PSM) of MOFs is a rout to modify MOF’s structure and its properties for desired applications.41, 42, 60, 61 Very recently, post-synthetic nodemetalation of MOFs has been attracted significant attention by the most notable research groups.57-59, 62-64 Bimetal-functionalized MOF nodes and their applications have been far less explored for advanced and innovative uses.3, 58, 62, 64
Quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -one derivatives, the most important classes of nitrogen-containing heterocycle compounds, possess a wide range of pharmacological and biological properties including antibacterial, antidiabetic, antiviral, antiinflammatory, phosphorylation inhibition, EGFR inhibition, and anticancer activities.65-71 Therefore, in addition to the widely existing quinazolinone derivatives in natural products, synthetic quinazolinones have also attracted much attention. Up to now, there have been some reports relating the synthesis of the quinazolinone pharmacophore from reaction of alcohols and 2-aminonbenzamide in the presence of catalytic systems such as Ru/Xantphos/Crotononitrile, $^{72}\mathrm{Ir},^{73}\mathrm{Pt}$ (in harsh condition),74 $\mathfrak{a}$ -MnO2/tert-butyl hydroperoxide (TBHP),75 Fe(BTC)/TBHP/DMSO,76 and Ni(II) complex/NaOtBu/xylene.77 The high cost of catalysts, the difficulty of recycling catalyst, their deactivation, the use of additive, and harsh reaction conditions are disadvantages of many of these systems. Therefore, introduction of low-cost, highly efficient and reusable catalysts as well as environmentally benign protocols are highly demanded for advanced organic transformations.
Hence, in this work, we report a facile one-step strategy involving in-situ node-metalation and activation process of PCN-222(Fe) using anhydrous $F e C l_{3} $ (without using HCl) to prepare a novel, and bioinspired MOF with high density of the iron active centers, $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}_{-222(\mathrm{Fe})}$ . Innovatively, the as-prepared MOF was applied, for the first time, in cooperative photocatalysis and Lewis acid catalysis for one-pot tandem synthesis of quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -ones from reaction of alcohols and 2-amonibenzamide under visible light irradiation using air or $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ as an oxidant without adding any additive in acetonitrile/water mixture as a solvent. The new MOF exhibits an excellent performance for the one-pot three-steps tandem reaction process (oxidationcyclization-oxidation) towards formation of the quinazolin- $.4(3H)$ -ones. Up to now, based on our knowledge, there is no report about the preparation of the nominal MOF and the tandem synthesis of quinazolin- $.4(3H)$ -ones from alcohols under mild and environmentally benign conditions using visible-light (or sunlight) under $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ atmosphere. The strategy involving the preparation of the MOF by using earth-abundant and non-toxic iron salts (in comparison with the previous works), the preparation of quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -ones in one-pot without using any additive, reusability of the solid, and notably the use of visible-light irradiation as an alternative source of energy address the requirements for sustainable energy production.
## 2. Experimental section
## Materials
All reagents were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich and used without further purification. The synthesis of activated PCN-222(Fe) $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF was based on HCl-treated/activated procedure described by Zhou’s group.56 FeTCPPCl was prepared according to a modification of previous procedure.56
## Characterization
Synthesis of 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin-iron(III) chloride (Fe(III)TCPPCl)
FeTCPPCl was prepared according to a modification of previous procedure.56 Methyl 4- formylbenzoate $(6.9\ \mathrm{g},0.042\ \mathrm{mol})$ ) was added to refluxing propionic acid $(\mathrm{110\mL})$ followed by the addition of pyrrole $(3\mathrm{\mL},0.043\mathrm{\mol}),$ ). The resultant solution was then refluxed for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ in darkness before cooling down to room temperature. The reaction mixture was stored overnight at about $-4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then the precipitate collected by suction-filtration, repeatedly washed with water, ethanol and methanol. The obtained purple solid was dried under vacuum to give 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-methoxycarbonylphenyl)porphyrin (TPPCOOMe, $21\%$ yield, $\lvert1.9\mathrm{\g}\right)$ . The obtained TPPCOOMe $(0.854\mathrm{g},1.0\mathrm{mmol})$ ) and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}{\bullet}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(2.5~\mathrm{g},12.8~\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of DMF and the mixture was refluxed for $\bar{6}\mathrm{~h~}$ . The mixture was then cooled to room temperature, and $150~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was added to that. The resulting precipitate was filtered off and washed with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (two times). This material was dissolved in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ , followed by washing with 1M HCl (three times) and water (two times). The organic phase was then dried over anhydrous magnesium sulfate and evaporated to afford quantitative dark brown crystals (5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-methoxycarbonylphenyl)porphyrin-iron(III) chloride, FeTPPCOOMe). The FeTPPCOOMe $(0.75~\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in THF ( $30~\mathrm{mL})$ and MeOH ( $30~\mathrm{mL}$ ) mixed solvent, followed by adding a solution of KOH $(2.6\ \mathrm{g},\ 46.95\ \mathrm{mmol})$ in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $20\ \mathrm{mL})$ . This reaction mixture was stirred and refluxed for $18\mathrm{~h~}$ before cooling down to room temperature. After that, THF and MeOH were evaporated under vacuum. Additional water was added to the resulting water phase and the mixture was stirred and heated until the solid was fully dissolved. The resulting solution was acidified with 1M HCl until no further precipitate was observed. Finally, the brown solid was filtered off and washed with water and then dried in vacuum to afford Fe(III)TCPPCl as the organic linker ( $60\%$ yield, $425\ \mathrm{mg}$ ).
Synthesis of Fe@PCN-222(Fe)
In a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ screw neck glass vial, $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ ( $70~\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.30 mmol) and benzoic acid $(2700\mathrm{mg})$ in $8~\mathrm{mL}$ of DEF ( $\mathrm{\Delta}N,N^{\prime}$ -diethylformamide) were ultrasonically dissolved. The prepared FeTCPPCl $(50\mathrm{mg},0.057\mathrm{mmol})$ was added to the solution and the vial was capped and kept at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under static conditions for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, dark brown crystals were centrifuged and the solvent was decanted off. Then fresh DMF $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ) was added to the vial and shaken. The precipitate was separated by centrifugation and washed with DMF several times to remove unreacted starting ligands and trapped benzoic acid. Then the resulting solid was left to soak in DMF for $12\mathrm{h}$ at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and the solution was replaced with fresh acetone ( $\mathrm{\bar{40}m L}$ ). The dark participate was washed with acetone (three times). Subsequently, the obtained crystals were dried at room temperature and then at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum for 12 to give the parent MOF. This procedure yielded the material denoted with ‘‘as-synthesized $\mathrm{PCN-}222(\mathrm{Fe})^{\cdot,}$ (formula: $\smash{(Z r_{6}(\mu_{3}-\$ $\mathrm{O)_{4}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)_{4}}}$ (benzoate)4(FeTCPPCl)-4)2) $(\mathbf{C}_{124}\mathbf{H}_{72}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}_{2}\mathbf{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{N}_{8}\mathbf{O}_{32}\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{r}_{6},~M w~=~2915.89)$ . The assynthesized PCN-222(Fe) $\mathrm{50~mg)}$ ) was shaken in DMF $\mathrm{12~mL}$ (in a $20\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial). Then, anhydrous $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ $(400\ \mathrm{mg},2.47\ \mathrm{mmol})$ was added to the resultant suspension, and the mixture was shaken and incubated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . After the mixture was cooled to room temperature, the crystalline powder was separated by centrifugation. Subsequently, the solid residue was washed with DMF $\mathrm{4}\times30\mathrm{mL}$ , over a 12-hour period) and then soaked in DMF ( $\mathrm{50~mL})$ at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove unreacted iron ions. The DMF was replaced with acetone several times over a 24-hour period. Finally, the solid was dried under vacuum at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ to give the bimetallic MOF based on octanuclear $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}$ cluster, denoted with $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}_{-222(\mathrm{Fe})}$ $38~\mathrm{~mg}$ , formula: $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{FeTCPPCl})_{2}(\mathrm{Fe})_{2})$ $\mathrm{(C_{96}H_{60}C l_{2}F e_{4}N_{8}O_{32}Z r_{6}}$ , $M w=2679.18$ ).
