## 3. Results and Discussion
## Characterization of the multifunctional MOF (Fe@PCN-222(Fe))
Fe@PCN-222(Fe) was achieved through a three-step synthetic strategy as shown in Scheme 1 and 2. Firstly: FeTCPPCl, as the organic linker, was obtained as dark brown solid via the following three steps (a-c); a) condensation reaction between methyl 4-formylbenzoate and pyrrole in refluxing propionic acid to give 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4- methoxycarbonylphenyl)porphyrin (TPPCOOMe), b) metalation of TPPCOOMe with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{2}$ towards formation of FeTPPCOOMe, and then c) hydrolysis of the obtained iron-porphyrin with KOH (see their analysis data in ESI, Fig. S1-S6), as given in Scheme 1. Later: as-synthesized PCN-222(Fe) was obtained by the solvothermal condition method according to the procedure reported previously (see the experimental section for details).56 Finally: $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}-222(\mathrm{Fe})$ was prepared by combination of as-synthesized PCN-222(Fe) and anhydrous $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ in DMF at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ \mathrm{h}$ , as indicated in scheme 1 and 2, see the experimental section for details (It should be noted that the functionalization of MOF at nodes has been reported by several MOF research groups, albeit by diverse deposition methods).3, 57-59, 62, 64
As shown in the Scheme 2 (a, b), the post-metalation of the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -node with iron ions results in new iron-oxo bonds on the nodes. The reaction of the as-synthesized PCN-222(Fe) and FeCl3 results in as both removing the benzoate ligands by in-situ HCl generation, and leaving eight terminal - ${\cdot}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligands on nodes $(\mathrm{[Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-O)_{4}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)_{4}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}}}]}$ as nodes) that are capable of coordination with $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ through the post-synthetic metalation process (The route is similar to that of reported by Farha et al. for Hf-based MOF).62 The nominal MOF consists of octanuclear $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}$ nodes that are connected by carboxylate groups of FeTCPPCl units (Scheme 2).
FT-IR spectrum of Fe@PCN-222(Fe) (Fig. S7) shows a broad band centered at 3420 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to a small fraction of -OH groups and physisorbed water trapped inside the network. The asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of the carboxylate groups are appeared at 1606 and $1417~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively. The band at $1540~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ is assigned to the imine group $\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{N}$ bond) of the metalloporphyrine. The iron-nitrogen and iron-oxygen peaks are observed as a broad band centered at about $660~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . In comparison to PCN-222(Fe) (which activated with HCl), the slight shift of the peak from 649 to $660~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was observed after the post-metalation (Figure S8). The bands related to the $\mathrm{Zr}$ -containing moiety are hardly detectable (Fig. S7). PXRD of the prepared MOFs revealed that the samples are isostructural to the pattern of PCN-222 MOF,55, 56 confirming the successful syntheses of the PCN-222(Fe) and $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN-}$ 222(Fe) (Fig. 1). The identical Bragg diffraction peaks at $2\theta=2.5,4.9,6.6,7.1,9.7$ and $9.9^{\mathrm{{o}}}$ are corresponded, respectively, to the (1 0 0), (2 0 0), (2 -1 1), (2 0 1), (4 0 0), (4 -2 1) Miller indices as observed for the simulated XRD pattern of PCN-222( $\mathrm{Fe)}$ (CCDC No. 893545).
The Zr6 node-functionalization of PCN-222(Fe) with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ was further investigated by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP–AES) analysis. ICP–AES analysis of the digested $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}-222(\mathrm{Fe})$ showed an iron loading of $6.9~\mathrm{wt}\%$ with a $\mathrm{Zr/Fe}$ ratio of 6:4. This is corresponded to four Fe ions per hexa-zirconium node $\mathrm{\small{[Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}\mathrm{-OH)_{8}(O H)_{8}}}}$ or $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}{-}O)_{4}(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}(O H)_{4}]}$ , in which the terminal - ${\cdot}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{OH}$ ligands coordinated to the two $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ ions via the post-synthetic metalation. It should be noted that longer reaction time did not increase the metal loading. Iron loading of $3.5~\mathrm{wt}\%$ and a $\mathrm{Zr/Fe}$ ratio of 6:2 were obtained for PCN-222(Fe), corresponding to two Fe(III)-porphyrin ligands per hexazirconium node. The results showed 2-fold increase in the amount of iron for $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}-222(\mathrm{Fe})$ compared to the pristine MOF, PCN-222(Fe), confirming the successful post-metalation.
Derived from experimental ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms revealed that $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN-}$ 222(Fe) has a Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $1740\ m^{2}\ {\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and a total pore volume of $1.22\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 2), which is agreement with $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ -functionalized PCN-222(Fe) nodes. The gradual increase of both BET surface area (from 1460 to $1740\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1})$ and pore volume $\mathrm{\from\0.89}$ to $1.22~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ from as-prepared PCN-222(Fe) to F $\mathsf{e}@\mathsf{P C N-}222(\mathsf{F e})$ is agreement with the removal of the benzoate molecules bounded to the $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ cluster,56, 58, 62 possibly caused by the in-situ HCl formed from reaction of ${}-\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligands on the node and $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ during the post-synthetic node-metalation. The isotherm of the MOF exhibits a characteristic type IV shape, similar to that of PCN-222(Fe),56 indicating the presence of the hexagonal mesochannels in addition to the microchannels in the framework, as shown in Fig. 3.
The morphology of Fe@PCN-222(Fe) particles was assessed by SEM images (Figures 4a,4b). The $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF prepared shows hexagonal-rod shapes with sizes ranging from 200 to $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter, being similar to that previously reported for PCN-222 MOF.21, 56 Further, energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) elemental mapping images of Fe@PCN-222(Fe) reveal obviously the extremely homogeneous distribution of C, N, O, Cl, Fe, and Zr species in the whole framework (Figures 4c-4j).
Diffuse reflectance UV-Vis spectroscopy (UV-Vis DRS) of the PCN-222(Fe) and $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}-222(\mathrm{Fe})$ are depicted in Fig. 5. As seen in the Fig., the UV-vis spectrum of Fe@PCN222(Fe) is nearly the same as that of the PCN-222(Fe), albeit with differing intensities which can result from the different iron loadings. The increased peak intensities observed around $300~\mathrm{nm}$ for Fe $@$ PCN-222(Fe) is consistent with the characteristic absorption peak at about $350\ \mathrm{nm}$ related to $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathbf{S}_{2}$ functionality (Fe complex) in UiO-66 MOF.78 These absorption bands (between $200{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ ) seen via this technique may attribute to a) large ligand-to-metal-cluster charge transfer (LMCT) within the MOF and b) charge transfer between the zirconium and the iron/metal-to-metal charge transfer (MMCT). The optical band gap energy of $1.65\ \mathrm{eV}$ was calculated for the MOF by using equation: $\mathrm{Eg}\left(\mathrm{eV}\right)=1240/$ (the absorption edge wavelength in nm from the spectrum).
Thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) in air is used to determine the thermal stability of these two MOFs (Fig. S9 in ESI). As shown in the Figs., the two materials are stable up to 500 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air (percent weight residual mass; obs: 66.6 wt. $\%$ for PCN-222(Fe) and $69.4\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ for $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}_{-222(\mathrm{Fe}))}$ , indicating their high thermal stability (Fig. S9). The gradual mass loss from ${\sim}100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is due to the release of the trapped solvent in the pores and the dehydration of the MOFs (obs: ${\sim}14~\mathrm{wt.}\%$ for PCN-222(Fe) and ${\sim}11$ wt. $\%$ for $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}_{-}222(\mathrm{Fe}))$ .
The photoluminescence (PL) spectra of the two samples (PCN-222(Fe) and Fe@PCN222(Fe)) are shown in Figure 6a-c. To cut-off the effect of exciting source reflectance, appropriate filters were place against incident beams. The Fig. 6a is corresponded to the excitation wavelength of $250~\mathrm{nm}$ . Obviously, the MOF samples reveal various ultraviolet and visible luminescence. The considerably quenching the intensity of luminescence of Fe@PCN222(Fe) can be due to fast capturing of photogenerated electrons from the conduction band minimum (CBM) of PCN-222(Fe) by the Fe trapping sites, which is indicated the electron transfer from the Z-MOF to Fe(III), suggesting electron/hole recombination inhibition in the MOF after the metalation. The trapping of photo-generated carriers by trap sites results in fast fluorescence decay as reported in literature.79 This fact is desirable especially in (photo)catalytic applications in which oxygen-containing reactive radicals are generated simply by interactions with the photogenerated electron/hole pairs in the MOF. By exciting the sample with the wavelength of $350\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 6b), qualitatively similar results are attained. Nevertheless, by applying a lower energy of excitation ( ${\bf\dot{400}}~\mathrm{nm})$ , the overall intensity of the plots is decreased implying that just a few number of electrons can transit between sub-levels (see Fig. 6c).
