In addition, after the metalation of PCN-222(Fe) with iron, the slight shift of two wavenumbers from 362 and $740~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, to 392 and $780~\mathrm{nm}$ were clearly observed (see in wide scan PL spectra of the two MOFs in SI, Figure S10). The results explain that iron incorporated into the Fe@PCN-224(Fe) via the post node-metalation can improve the efficiency of electron-hole pair separation, suggesting that the new MOF can be utilized as an excellent candidate for light-emitting plans.
X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) analysis78 was further employed for the characterization of the respective MOF (Fig. 7a,b). The XPS survey spectra of the MOFs show the presence of elements of carbon (C 1s, binding energy at $284.5\mathrm{eV}$ ), oxygen (O 1s), zirconium $(\mathrm{Zr~}3\mathrm{d})$ , and iron (Fe 2p) (Fig. 7a). Notably, Fe@PCN-222(Fe) showed an increase in the intensities of the peaks at around 711 and $724{\mathrm{~eV}}$ related to Fe 2p compared to the bare MOF (Fig. 7b), which can be evidence of the formation of O-Fe bonds and the presence of the corresponding Fe(III) species78 after the post-metalation. This is in agreement well with the results of FT-IR, PXRD, BET surface area, UV-vis DRS, PL, and ICP analyses.
## Catalytic activity
In our previous studies, an efficient photocatalytic system for one-pot tandem80 oxidative Passerini three-component reaction was reported.10 In continues of our work, Fe@PCN-222(Fe) (or Fe@MOF-545), for the first time, was designed and prepared to increase the potential photocatalytic and catalytic activity of the biomimetic 3D framework, PCN-222(Fe), under visible light irradiation towards an environmentally benign and sustainable organic transformation. Herein, after the characterization of the respective MOF, it was initially tested for the synthesis of 2-phenyl-quinazline- $4(3H)$ -one through a one-pot tandem reaction between benzyl alcohol and 2-aminobenzamide, as a model reaction, under visible-light irradiation in air (air bubbling) as the oxygen source (Table 1). The product was achieved in $69\%$ yield after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ under the aerobic photo-oxidation reaction (Table 1, Entry 1). It should be noted the sunlight radiation as the light source results in $72\%$ yield of the product after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . No product was observed in the dark or without the catalyst (Entries 2 and 3). When the reaction was carried out using $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , as an oxidant, the reaction time was decreased from $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ to $32\mathrm{~h~}$ and the yield of the product increased from $69\%$ to $75\%$ (Entry 4). Therefore, the following examines were carried out using $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ , as a terminal oxidant. The amount of the catalyst was then optimized and found to be $30\ \mathrm{\mg}$ , reaching the highest yield of the product in a short reaction time with lowconsumption of the catalyst (Entries 4-6). Control experiments under the same conditions using the related catalysts such as PCN-222(Fe), Fe(BTC) (as a Fe-based MOF), UiO-66 (as a Zrbased MOF), FeTCPPCl (homogeneous catalyst with the amount of iron-porphyrin from the MOF), $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ showed that the activity of the new-designed multifunctional MOF was superior than the mentioned-catalysts, confirming again the successful incorporation of iron into the MOF (Entries 7-12). Furthermore, the results indicate that the porphyrin linker (as main catalytic sites) and $Z\mathbf{r}$ -oxo cluster as well as the Fe sites of nodes could act as active sites for the tandem reaction. A blank experiment using UiO-66 (consisting of Zr-oxo cluster), $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ (as the iron precursor as used for the preparation of the MOF), and FeTCPPCl (as the corresponding linker in the MOF) shows that the material produces also the product, but with much higher reaction time and lower yield ( $48\mathrm{~h~}$ was only $52\%$ yield) (Entry 13). The result confirmed the unique properties of the MOF as single-site solid catalysts (Homogenous porphyrin can destroy via formation of $\mu$ -oxo dimers and self-oxidation under the oxidation reaction as mentioned before). On the other hand, mesoporosity of the MOF, as a micro-meso-structured reactor, may result in more penetration of substrates into the framework, and providing more accessible active sites for the substrates, thereby, increasing its performance in the reaction.
After that, the reaction in the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}-222(\mathrm{Fe})$ was studied in solvents (acetonitrile, toluene, water, and acetonitrile/water mixture), and in solvent-free conditions as given in Table 1 (Entries 14-17). As it can be seen there, the acetonitrile/water mixture $(4{:}1\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v})$ was chosen as the optimal solvent for the photo-oxidation reaction in term of the highest yield of the product gained in this solvent and in view of environmentally friendly synthetic routes (Table 1, Entry 15). The improvement may be due to good solubility of the starting materials and products in the solvent, high stability of the reaction media towards oxidation under the reaction conditions, and water-assisted activation of $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ in the solvent mixture.81-83 It should be mentioned that the more water results in the decrease of the yield of the product. The obtained data reveal that the catalytic activity of the MOF is affected by various parameters such as the oxidant, light source, catalyst loading, and solvent. Also, the results confirmed that the (photo-) catalytic activity of the nominal MOF (after the post-cluster metalation) is better than homogeneous zirconium and iron salts and Fe-porphyrin complex, as well as other related heterogeneized catalysts.
To investigate the scope of the reaction, different substrates containing electron-donating and electron-withdrawing groups (such as $o$ -Me, $p$ -Me, $p$ -Cl, and $\scriptstyle p-\mathrm{NO}_{2}.$ ) in the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}_{-222(\mathrm{Fe})}$ under the optimized condition were tested (Table 2). As shown in the Table, quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -ones were achieved in moderate-good yields $(60-81\%)$ ), and turn over frequency (TOF) between $0.0960.15\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ , demonstrating the versatility of this method for the diverse groups. The turnover frequency (TOF) was calculated based on the number of the iron and zirconium ions in ${\mathrm{Fe}}@\operatorname{PCN}-222(\operatorname{Fe}).$ .
Reaction conditions: 2-aminobenzamide $\mathrm{0.5~mmol}$ ), substituted benzyl alcohol (1.5 mmol), Fe@PCN222(Fe) ( $30~\mathrm{mg}$ , equivalent to $0.044\mathrm{mmol}$ Fe and 0.067 mmol Zr) and acetonitrile/water (4:1 v/v, 4-5 ml) under LED light irradiation.a Yields refer to the isolated pure products.b TOF (turnover frequency) $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ TON per time (h); $\mathrm{TON}=$ yield $(\%)$ )/catalyst $(\mathrm{mol}\%)$ ).c Reusability of catalyst in the second run.d Reusability of catalyst in the third run.
The catalytic activity and reusability of Fe@PCN-222(Fe) was evaluated by performing three consecutive reactions for the tandem synthesis of 2-phenyl-quinazline- $4(3H)$ -one under the optimized condition, uses of benzyl alcohol and 2-aminobenzamide as starting materials (Table 2, Entry 1). As shown in the Table, no significant changes were observed in the reaction time and yield after the three consecutive runs. The stability of the photocatalyst was firstly assessed by FT-IR, PXRD, AAS, and SEM. No changes or additional peaks were observed when the FT-IR spectrum of the fresh sample was compared with the spectrum of the reused sample (Figure S7). The crystallinity of the reused $\mathrm{Fe}@\mathrm{PCN}-222(\mathrm{Fe})$ was checked by PXRD (Fig. 1), demonstrating that the crystallinity of the framework is conserved under the reaction conditions. Atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) analysis of the filtrate at the end of the reaction reveals a negligible iron leaching less than $1\mathrm{wt}\%$ of the initial Fe present in the nominal catalyst, which is considered negligible (the Fe leaching after the second cycle was lower than $0.3\mathrm{wt}\%$ and remained unchanged). Furthermore, the SEM images indicate that the morphology of the resultant photocatalyst is preserved under the reaction conditions (Figure S10). All these data confirm that the MOF is stable under the reaction condition.
The heterogeneous nature of the catalyst was then assessed by carrying out the reaction between benzyl alcohol, and 2-aminobenzamide in the presence of the $\mathsf{F e}@\mathsf{P C N-}222(\mathsf{F e})$ under the previous-mentioned conditions. The solid catalyst was filtered after $17\mathrm{~h~}$ reaction time and then the reaction containing the unreacted starting materials allowed to continue without the catalyst. The progress in the absence of catalyst was negligible (only $40\%$ of the yield was observed), confirming the heterogeneity of the catalytic process.
Scheme 3. Proposed mechanism for one-pot tandem synthesis of quinazloine- $4(3H)$ -one from alcohols using Fe@PCN-222(Fe) under visible LED light irradiation.
To investigate the proposed mechanism, the reaction between benzyl alcohol, and 2- aminobenzamide was carried out under the optimized conditions. As shown in Table 1, when the light was applied for the reaction, quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -one as the product was formed after $32\mathrm{~h~}$ . Aldehyde was observed during the reaction (Scheme 3). No intermediate or product or byproduct such as benzaldehyde or quinazolin- $4(1H)$ -one or quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -one was obtained without using the light irradiation. The reaction between benzaldehyde (instead of the benzyl alcohol) and 2-aminobenzamide under the optimized conditions without the light irradiation generated the aminal intermediate (quinazolin- $.4(1H)$ -one) after $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , while with the light the final product was obtained after $12\mathrm{~h~}$ (Scheme S1). These results confirmed again that the MOF acts as both a Lewis acid and redox catalyst (merging visible-light photocatalysis and metal catalysis for the synthesis). In addition, the experiments demonstrate that benzaldehyde and quinazolin$4(1H)$ -one are the reaction intermediates. Furthermore, the control tests indicate that the condensation of aldehyde with 2-aminobenzaimde to quinazolin- $4(1H)$ -one (the aminal intermediate), and its oxidation to quinazolin- $4(3H)$ -one are fast and the photo-oxidation of alcohol to aldehyde (step 1) is the rate-limiting step in the reaction.
To issue that the catalysis occurs frequently in the interior of the Fe@PCN-222(Fe) pores rather than on the exterior surface, the molecular size of starting materials and the products was calculated (Table S1). The measured diameter of apertures of the nominal MOF from the poresize distribution and the calculated molecular size demonstrated that the catalysis can take place in the MOF pores. The mesoporous channels enable starting materials to diffuse in the pores, then reacted with together through the active sites towards the product formation. Then the product can drive out from the framework for the following cycles.
